Z||000000||IwannaIRM||08-17-2001||12:15 PM||||I read one of your earlier replies on towing a Fiero with another Fiero.<P>Which trailer hitch did you use?  The draw-tite is only rated at 2000lbs throught the Fiero Store.<P>What kind of gas mileage did you get(for planning stops)?<P>Was your tow vehicle an auto or manual? I4 or V6?<P>I am thinking of doing a similar towing situation and just need to know what to expect or prepare for.<P>------------------<BR>AIM - SWags2933<P>84 Fiero SE (being modified)<BR>88 Formula||||reg||5
Z||000001||mrfiero||08-17-2001||03:23 PM||||I have successfully towed many Fieros with a Fiero, even though there are many who either don't believe it or claim it is unsafe.<P>I use the Fiero Stores hitch and have had no problems with it.  Most of my towing has been with an '88 GT V-6, auto., but I just did a trip with a 5-sp. '88 GT.  I wouldn't use a 4-cyl......it may do OK, but you can really use the extra grunt of the V-6.<P>I towed with a dolly.....a trailer is too much for the Fiero to handle.  Always tow with the rear wheels on the dolly (car being towed is facing backwards).  This puts the majority of the weight where it needs to be.  Once you are cruising you will hardly notice you are towing.<P>The biggest factor is braking.  I have only used '88s to tow with, so if you will be using an earlier year you should factor in some additional room when planning stops.  The '88 brakes are less prone to fading, etc., but as long as you don't need to make any panic stops you'll be fine.  Just plan your stops ahead of time and down shift (engine brake) when you can.  <P>With the auto. I would shift to L2 then to L1 before even touching the brake pedal.......this slowed me down enough without using up the brakes.<P>As for mileage......I got about 15MPG with the auto. and 17-18 MPG with the stick.  I preferred the auto., since there was no clutch to worry about burning up.<P>Bring along a 2 gallon gas can and you'll have no problems making it to a gas station.<P>How far are you planning on towing?  Like I said before, I have towed with a Fiero on numerous occasions......most have been 1000+ mile trips.  Denver to Sacramento to Denver (2500+ mi.); Denver to Portland, Oregon to Portland, Maine to Denver (5000+ mi.); Denver to Indy to Denver (2500 mi.); Denver to Portland, Maine to Detroit to Denver (5000 mi.), etc., etc.<P>I have logged over 20K miles towing cars with a Fiero and have had no major issues.  I did lose a catalytic convertor on one trip and a coil on another, but that could have happened anytime.  I was getting 9MPG after the cat went (Ouch!).....that happened last year as gas prices soared to over $2/gal in the miodwest (I was just south of Chicago when it pooped out).  It was an expensive trip home.....I ran out of gas every 100 miles!  <P>At any rate, it can be done....just make sure your Fiero is tuned up and everything is properly adjusted before you leave.<P>E-mail me if you have any other questions.     ||||reg||1
